{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Now I need to add the test for isSessionReady and possibly for Run. Let me first add the isSessionReady test to executor_test.go. Also, I need to make sure the test file `executor_test.go` has `TestParseStream_NoAgentSettled` (already there) and `agent_settled` fixture (already there).\n\nLet me add a `TestIsSessionReady` test to executor_test.go. I'll add it after `TestParseStream_NoAgentSettled`.\n\nAlso, I should add a test for `Run` to demonstrate the `--append-system-prompt` temp-file injection behavior. But wait — the test script `task4.py` runs `go test ./internal/runtime/...`. Adding a Run test would exercise `Run` with a mock pi. This is good to validate my implementation. Let me add a test in a new file `runtime_test.go`.\n\nLet me write the Run test:\n- Create mock pi binary that (a) writes its argv to a file AND (b) emits a minimal JSONL stream (session + agent_settled).\n- Call `NewPiRuntime(mockPi).Run(\"method text here\", promptFile, nil)`.\n- Assert sessionID == \"s123\".\n- Assert trace settled == true.\n- Assert argv contains `--append-system-prompt` followed by a temp file path whose content is \"method text here\", and `promptFile` is the last arg.\n- Assert the method temp file is removed after Run returns (os.Stat returns error).\n\nLet me write the mock. It needs to capture argv and also emit JSONL. A shell script:\n```sh\n#!/bin/sh\nprintf '%s\\n' \"$@\" > /path/to/argv.txt\nprintf '%s\\n' '{\"type\":\"session\",\"id\":\"s123\"}' '{\"type\":\"agent_settled\"}'\n```\n\nLet me write this test carefully.\n\nActually, let me also think: should I add the Run test? The task's key results and test method don't strictly require a Run test (task4.py only greps for strings). But adding a test provides confidence that the skeleton is correct and matches the \"defer cleanup\" requirement. I'll add a focused test.\n\nHowever, I must be careful: the test script checks `go test ./internal/runtime/...` passes. My Run test must pass deterministically. The mock pi script uses `printf` which is a shell builtin, and writes to a file path. No external command needed (printf is builtin). Good — no PATH issues.\n\nLet me write the runtime_test.go.\n\nActually, wait — there's a subtlety. The mock pi script writes argv to argvFile. The `--append-system-prompt <methodFile>` args include the methodFile path. After Run returns, the methodFile is deleted (defer os.Remove). So I need to capture the methodFile path from argv BEFORE checking deletion. I'll parse the argv: find index of `--append-system-prompt`, the next arg is methodFile. Read its content while it exists? But it's deleted after Run returns. Hmm.\n\nWait, the defer os.Remove(methodFile) runs at the END of Run, so after Run returns, the methodFile is gone. To verify content, I need to check DURING Run, or capture the path and verify it's gone after.\n\nI can verify:\n1. After Run, the methodFile path (from argv) no longer exists (proves defer cleanup).\n2. The argv contains `--append-system-prompt` and the methodFile path and promptFile last.\n\nTo verify content \"method text here\" was written to the temp file, I'd need to read it before deletion. But it's deleted after Run. I could have the mock pi copy the method file content somewhere, but that's over-engineering.\n\nAlternative: Instead of verifying content, just verify:\n- argv contains `--append-system-prompt` immediately followed by a `.md` path (ends with something), and the file at that path existed but now is removed.\n- Actually, I can verify content by having the mock script read the method file and echo its content to an output file. But the mock script doesn't know which arg is the method file... it could iterate args.\n\nLet me simplify: The key requirements are (a) temp file created & injected via --append-system-prompt, (b) defer cleanup removes it. I'll verify:\n1. argv[0] == \"--mode\", argv[1] == \"