{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"The Run test failed: method file content is empty. This means the mock script's method content capture isn't working. Let me debug.\n\nThe issue is likely the shell script's `while IFS= read -r line; do ... done < \"$method\"` combined with the `for a in \"$@\"` loop. Wait, actually the issue might be that the method content capture happens but then... Let me think.\n\nActually wait, the argv was recorded correctly (the test got past argv check since it reached line 118 which is the method content check). Let me check: the test failed at line 118 `method file content: want \"the method text\", got \"\"`. So `methodContentFile` exists but is empty (or read succeeded with empty content).\n\nWait, actually `os.ReadFile(methodContentFile)` returned nil error (otherwise it would have `t.Fatalf` at line 117). So the file exists but is empty.\n\nLet me look at the shell script logic more carefully:\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/sh\nprintf '%s\\n' \"$@\" > \"ARGV_FILE\"\nprev=\"\"\nmethod=\"\"\nfor a in \"$@\"; do\n  if [ \"$prev\" = \"--append-system-prompt\" ]; then method=\"$a\"; fi\n  prev=\"$a\"\ndone\nif [ -n \"$method\" ]; then\n  while IFS= read -r line; do printf '%s\\n' \"$line\"; done < \"$method\" > \"METHOD_CONTENT_FILE\"\nfi\necho '{\"type\":\"session\",\"id\":\"s123\"}'\necho '{\"type\":\"agent_settled\"}'\n```\n\nHmm. The `for a in \"$@\"` loop iterates over args. But wait — the issue: `$@` is consumed? No, `$@` remains accessible. Actually `for a in \"$@\"` iterates correctly.\n\nBut wait — the `while IFS= read -r line; do printf '%s\\n' \"$line\"; done < \"$method\"` — this reads from the method file. The method file content is \"the method text\" (one line, no trailing newline? `f.WriteString(\"the method text\")` writes without trailing newline). So `read -r line` reads \"the method text\" and then `printf '%s\\n' \"$line\"` writes \"the method text\\n\". That should work.\n\nWait, but maybe the issue is the `>` redirection truncates the file, and the `while` loop reads from `$method` (the method file) while writing to `$methodContentFile`. That's fine, different files.\n\nHmm, let me check: is `$method` actually being set correctly? The argv includes `--append-system-prompt <methodFile>`. So in the for loop, when `$a` is the methodFile and `$prev` is `--append-system-prompt`, then `method=\"$a\"` is set. Then `prev=\"$a\"` (methodFile).\n\nWait, but there's a subtle issue: the argv is recorded as one arg per line. The methodFile path might contain special characters? No, t.TempDir() paths are like `/tmp/TestPiRuntimeRun_AppendSystemPrompt123456/001/...` — no spaces.\n\nActually, wait. Let me check the actual issue by running the mock script manually. Let me create a debug version.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The issue might be simpler: `printf '%s\\n' \"$line\"` — when `$line` contains `%` characters... no, \"the method text\" has no `%`.\n\nWait, actually there might be an issue with how I write the method text. In `Run`, I do `f.WriteString(methodText)`. The methodText is \"the method text\". Then the file has \"the method text\" (11 chars, no newline).\n\nThen the mock does `while IFS= read -r line; do printf '%s\\n' \"$line\"; done < \"$method\"`.\n\nHmm wait — actually, the problem could be that `read -r line` reads the line but because there's no trailing newline, `read` returns non-zero (EOF) but still populates `$line`. Actually, in POSIX sh, `read` returns success if it reads at least one character even without trailing newline? Let me recall: `read` returns 0 if it reads data, even if there's no trailing newline (it returns non-zero only on EOF with no data). So the loop body should execute once with line=\"the method text\".\n\nHmm, but the result is empty. Let me actually debug by running the script manually.\n\nLet me write a quick debug."}}
