{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"`gofmt -l` lists files that are NOT properly formatted. Many of these are cmd files I edited (sed may have introduced formatting issues, e.g., import ordering). Wait, actually the sed only changed the import path string, not formatting. But `gofmt -l` shows these files need formatting — this might be pre-existing (the files were already not gofmt-clean) OR my sed changed something.\n\nLet me check what gofmt would change in one of my edited files. Actually, more importantly, the import block in these files: I changed `\"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/agent/piagent\"` → `\"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/runtime\"`. The import ordering (alphabetical) might now be wrong: `internal/runtime` vs `internal/config` vs `internal/executor`. Since `runtime` comes after `executor` alphabetically? No: `config` < `executor` < `git` < `parser` < `prompt` < `runtime` < `workspace`. So `runtime` should be sorted after `prompt` and before `workspace`.\n\nWait, but the import was previously `piagent` which sorts after `parser`... let me check. Actually `agent/piagent` sorts under `agent/` which comes FIRST (before config). So previously the import order was:\n```\n\"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/agent/piagent\"\n\"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/config\"\n...\n```\nNow it's:\n```\n\"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/config\"\n\"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/executor\"\n...\n\"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/runtime\"\n```\nBut where did I leave `runtime`? The sed replaced the `piagent` import line in-place, so the import line stays in the SAME position (first), now reading `\"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/runtime\"` in the first position, but alphabetically `runtime` should be near the end.\n\nSo gofmt wants to reorder the imports. That's why these files are now \"unformatted\". I need to run gofmt -w to fix the import ordering.\n\nWait, but `internal/cmd/cli_integration_test.go`, `ctrl.go`, `doing_test.go`, `dream.go`, `human_loop_test.go`, `learning.go`, `learning_test.go` (not shown, hmm), `plan_test.go`, etc. — some of these I didn't edit (doing_test.go, cli_integration_test.go). Let me check: were these already unformatted BEFORE my changes?\n\n`doing_test.go` — I did NOT edit it (no piagent reference). But gofmt -l flags it. That means doing_test.go was ALREADY unformatted before my changes. Let me verify by checking git status / diff.\n\nActually, let me check: the files gofmt flags are mostly cmd files. Some I edited (ctrl.go, doing.go, dream.go, easy.go, human_loop.go, learning.go, plan.go, tools_doing_check.go, tools_plan_check.go, tools_test.go, human_loop_test.go). Others I did NOT edit (cli_integration_test.go, doing_test.go).\n\nSo there's a mix: some were already unformatted (pre-existing), and my sed introduced import-order issues in the files I edited.\n\nLet me just run `gofmt -w` on ALL files I changed + the new files, to ensure they're properly formatted. But I should be careful NOT to reformat files I didn't touch (that would pollute the diff). However, `gofmt -l` showing doing_test.go and cli_integration_test.go suggests those were already unformatted. I should NOT touch them (avoid scope creep).\n\nLet me identify exactly which files I changed:\n1. internal/runtime/*.go (all new/copied) — need gofmt.\n2. internal/config/config.go, loader.go, loader_test.go — need gofmt.\n3. internal/cmd/ files I sed-edited: tools_learning_check.go, dream.go, easy.go, doing.go, tools_plan_check.go, learning.go, human_loop.go, tools_init_pi.go, tools_doing_check.go, ctrl.go, tools_theme.go, plan.go (source) + plan_test.go, human_loop_test.go, tools_test.go, learning_test.go (test files, comments).\n\nLet me run gofmt -w on exactly those files. Actually, simpler: run gofmt -w on the specific files I changed. Let me get the list of files I changed via git diff.\n\nActually, let me just check git status to see modified files, then g